PART 4 — Block diagram, section B: loop 1 (send)
4.1 Structures
1.	Functions » Programming » Structures » While Loop — drag out a large rectangle to the right of section A
2.	Functions » Programming » Structures » Event Structure — place it inside, on the left. Leave room to its right.
4.2 Create three output tunnels
Wire these out of the right edge of the Event Structure. You'll create them by wiring from inside a case to the border.
•	MSGID (U8)
•	Payload (U8 array)
•	Send? (boolean)
Critical: right-click each of the three tunnels → tick Use Default If Unwired. Without this, LabVIEW forces you to wire all three in every single case, including the timeout case.
4.3 Add the event cases
Right-click the Event Structure border → Add Event Case. In the dialog, pick the control on the left and Value Change on the right.
Case: Set Mode → Value Change
•	U8 constant 0 → MSGID tunnel
•	Functions » Programming » Array » Array Constant, drop a U8 numeric inside, set the single element to 65 (that's 0x41, ASCII A) → Payload tunnel
•	TRUE constant → Send? tunnel
Case: Home All → Value Change
•	U8 constant 145 (0x91) → MSGID
•	Empty U8 array constant → Payload
•	TRUE → Send?
Case: Read Positions → Value Change
Same, MSGID 178 (0xB2), empty payload.
Case: Status Streaming → Value Change
•	U8 constant 1 → MSGID
•	Wire the Status Streaming terminal (LabVIEW puts a NewVal terminal on the event node — use that) → Functions » Programming » Boolean » Boolean To (0,1) → To Unsigned Byte → Build Array (one input) → Payload
•	TRUE → Send?
Case: Move Fins → Value Change
This one has real content. Build it in this order:
1.	Functions » Programming » Array » Build Array — resize to 4 inputs. Wire Fin 1 (deg) … Fin 4 (deg) terminals into it. Output is a 4-element DBL array.
2.	Another Build Array, 4 inputs. Wire Move Fin 1? … Move Fin 4?. Output is a 4-element boolean array.
3.	Functions » Programming » Structures » For Loop. Wire both arrays into it (both tunnels auto-index, so N becomes 4 automatically).
4.	Inside the For Loop, place a Case Structure driven by the boolean element. TRUE case (this fin moves): 
o	indexed degrees → Multiply by numeric constant 1241.6
o	→ Functions » Programming » Numeric » Round To Nearest
o	→ To Long Integer (Programming » Numeric » Conversion)
o	→ Add numeric constant 8388608
o	→ Functions » Programming » Numeric » Quotient & Remainder, other input 65536. The quotient is byte 0.
o	the remainder → another Quotient & Remainder, other input 256. Quotient is byte 1, remainder is byte 2.
o	Put To Unsigned Byte on all three
o	Build Array with 3 inputs, in order byte0, byte1, byte2
FALSE case (don't move this fin): 
o	U8 Array Constant containing 0, 0, 0
5.	The Case Structure output goes to the For Loop's right edge as an auto-indexing tunnel. You now have a 4 × 3 two-dimensional array.
6.	Functions » Programming » Array » Reshape Array — first dimension input 12. (Drag the node to show only one dimension input.) Output is a flat 12-byte array.
7.	That → Payload tunnel. U8 constant 144 (0x90) → MSGID. TRUE → Send?.
Case: Start Test → Value Change
1.	Build Array, 4 inputs, wire Test Fin 1? … Test Fin 4?
2.	→ Functions » Programming » Boolean » Boolean Array To Number → To Unsigned Byte. That's your fin-select bitmask byte.
3.	Test Type terminal → To Unsigned Byte. That's the test-type byte.
4.	Repeat steps 1–6 of the Move Fins case using Test Deg 1 … Test Deg 4, except skip the Case Structure — every fin is always encoded here. You get 12 bytes.
5.	Build Array, 3 inputs, right-click it → tick Concatenate Inputs. Wire: fin-select byte, test-type byte, the 12-byte array. Result: 14 bytes.
6.	→ Payload. U8 constant 147 (0x93) → MSGID. TRUE → Send?.
Case: STOP → Value Change
•	Wire a TRUE constant to a fourth output tunnel called Stop? (also set Use Default If Unwired)
•	Wire Stop? to the While Loop's stop terminal
4.4 The shared send chain
Place this to the right of the Event Structure, still inside the While Loop. Every case feeds into it — this is what saves you from six copies.
1.	Functions » Programming » Structures » Case Structure, selector wired to the Send? tunnel
2.	FALSE case: empty. Do nothing.
3.	TRUE case, wire left to right: 
o	Payload → Functions » Programming » Array » Array Size → To Unsigned Byte → this is MSGLEN
o	Build Array, 5 inputs, right-click → Concatenate Inputs. In order: U8 constant 235 (0xEB), U8 constant 144 (0x90), MSGID, MSGLEN, Payload
o	that array → Checksum.vi (place via Functions » Select a VI…)
o	Build Array, 2 inputs, Concatenate Inputs ticked: the array from the previous Build Array, then the checksum byte
o	→ Functions » Programming » String » String/Array/Path Conversion » Byte Array To String
o	→ Functions » Instrument I/O » Serial » VISA Write (write buffer input)
o	branch the string to the TX Hex indicator so you can see what went out
4.	Wire the VISA resource from section A into VISA Write, and thread the error wire through
4.5 Timeout case
Select the Timeout case in the Event Structure and set the timeout terminal (top-left of the structure) to a numeric constant -1. The loop then sleeps until you click something — no CPU burn.
4.6 Test what you have
Run it. Click Set Mode. Look at TX Hex — it must read:
EB 90 00 01 41 3B
If it does, your entire transmit path is correct.
