PART 5 — Block diagram, section C: loop 2 (receive)
Place a second While Loop below loop 1, not inside it. They run in parallel.
5.1 Read whatever arrived
1.	Functions » Instrument I/O » Serial » VISA Bytes at Serial Port — wire the VISA resource in. Its output is a byte count.
2.	Functions » Programming » Comparison » Greater? — count vs numeric constant 0
3.	Case Structure on that result: 
o	TRUE: Functions » Instrument I/O » Serial » VISA Read, wire the byte count to its byte count input. Output string → Functions » Programming » String » String/Array/Path Conversion » String To Byte Array
o	FALSE: empty U8 Array Constant
4.	Functions » Programming » Timing » Wait (ms) inside the loop, constant 5
5.2 The buffer
1.	Right-click the left edge of loop 2 → Add Shift Register
2.	Wire an empty U8 Array Constant to the left shift register, from outside the loop
3.	Build Array, 2 inputs, Concatenate Inputs ticked: left shift register first, then the new bytes from 5.1
This shift register is the one piece of state you can't avoid. A serial read returns whatever bytes happened to arrive — often half a message, sometimes two and a half. Whatever's left over has to survive until the next read.
5.3 The parser
Place a While Loop inside loop 2. Give it one shift register, initialised from the concatenated buffer above.
Build it in two stages. Get stage 1 working before adding stage 2.
Stage 1 — assume the buffer starts at EB 90
Case Structure A, selector = Array Size(buffer) → Greater Or Equal? → 5
•	FALSE: wire buffer straight through to the right shift register. TRUE constant → parser loop stop terminal.
•	TRUE: continue below.
Inside A's TRUE case:
•	Index Array(buffer, 3) → To Long Integer → Add 5 → this is need
•	Case Structure B, selector = Array Size(buffer) ≥ need 
o	FALSE: buffer through unchanged. TRUE → stop terminal. (More bytes are still coming.)
o	TRUE: 
	Functions » Programming » Array » Array Subset — array = buffer, index 0, length = need - 1 → Checksum.vi → computed checksum
	Index Array(buffer, need - 1) → received checksum
	Equal? on the two
	Case Structure C on that comparison 
	TRUE: Index Array(buffer, 2) → MSGID. Array Subset(buffer, 4, need - 5) → payload. Send both into the decoder (Part 6). Increment Frames RX.
	FALSE: increment Checksum Errors.
	Array Subset(buffer, need) — no length wired, takes everything from there on → right shift register. FALSE → stop terminal, so the loop goes round again and picks up any further complete messages already buffered.
Stage 2 — add resync
Once stage 1 works, insert one more case between A and B.
Inside A's TRUE case, before anything else:
•	Index Array(buffer, 0) → Equal? → U8 constant 235
•	Index Array(buffer, 1) → Equal? → U8 constant 144
•	Functions » Programming » Boolean » And on the two results
•	Case Structure, selector = that And 
o	FALSE: Array Subset(buffer, 1) → right shift register (drops one byte). FALSE → stop terminal, so it keeps hunting.
o	TRUE: everything from stage 1 goes in here.
That's the whole sync-word mechanism: step forward one byte at a time until you find EB 90, then trust the checksum to confirm it wasn't a coincidence.
