PART 6 — The decoder
Inside case C's TRUE branch, place a Case Structure with MSGID as the selector.
Type the case labels in decimal. LabVIEW's case selector doesn't take hex:
Message	Type this	Hex
Status	154	0x9A
Read Positions reply	130	0x82
Home All reply	97	0x61
Set Mode reply	16	0x10
Functional test reply	99	0x63
Make one case the Default so unknown IDs don't error out — wire the payload to RX Hex there.
6.1 A reusable 3-byte decoder
You need this pattern several times. Build it once inside a For Loop:
•	Array Subset(payload, offset, count) → Reshape Array to n × 3
•	into a For Loop, auto-indexing the rows
•	inside: Index Array the row at 0, 1, 2 → To Long Integer on each
•	byte0 × 65536 + byte1 × 256 + byte2
•	Subtract 8388608
•	Multiply 0.000805 → degrees
•	auto-indexed output → array of n angles
Endianness: this assumes byte 0 is the most significant. The document never states it. If your positions come out looking random in Part 7 step 5, swap the multipliers so byte 2 gets × 65536 and byte 0 gets × 1. Because it's all in this one For Loop, that's a one-place edit.
6.2 Case 154 — status message (74 bytes)
•	Positions: Array Subset(payload, 0, 24) through the 3-byte decoder with Reshape to 8 × 3 → 8 angles. Array Subset(result, 0, 4) → the four Current n (deg) indicators (use Index Array with 4 outputs — drag the node taller). Array Subset(result, 4, 4) → the four Cmd n (deg) indicators. Also wire the full 8-element array straight to Position Chart.
•	Currents: Array Subset(payload, 24, 8) → Reshape Array to 4 × 2 → For Loop → byte0 × 256 + byte1 → Motor Currents array indicator
•	Status word: Index Array(payload, 32) and (payload, 33) → byte0 × 256 + byte1 → Functions » Programming » Boolean » Number To Boolean Array → Array Subset(0, 12) → Status Bits
•	PWM: Array Subset(payload, 34, 8) → same 2-byte pattern → PWM
6.3 Cases 130 and 97 — position replies (12 bytes)
Array Subset(payload, 0, 12) → 3-byte decoder with Reshape to 4 × 3 → the four Current n (deg) indicators.
6.4 Case 16 — Set Mode reply
Nothing to decode. Wire the payload to RX Hex so you can see the 41 come back.
6.5 Case 99 — functional test reply (33 bytes)
•	Index Array(payload, 0) → end flag → Not Equal? 0 → Test Running LED
•	Array Subset(payload, 1, 3) → 3-byte decoder → commanded Fin 1
•	Array Subset(payload, 4, 3) → 3-byte decoder → achieved Fin 1
•	Build Array, 2 inputs (not concatenate) → Test Chart
That gives you commanded vs achieved on one chart, which is the plot that actually tells you whether the actuator is tracking. Add the other fins later using the offsets in the message table — remember the currents sit in the order fin 2, fin 1, fin 3.
6.6 Stopping loop 2
Right-click the STOP terminal on the block diagram → Create » Local Variable. Drag the local into loop 2 (it defaults to Read mode, which is what you want). Wire it to loop 2's stop terminal.
This is why STOP had to be Switch When Pressed — a latch button resets after the first read and loop 2 would never see it.
