PART 7 — Section D and first run
7.1 Close the port
To the right of both loops: Functions » Instrument I/O » Serial » VISA Close, then Functions » Programming » Dialog & User Interface » Simple Error Handler.
Wire both loops' error wires into a Merge Errors node (Programming » Dialog & User Interface » Merge Errors) first, then into Close.
7.2 Clean up
Ctrl+U runs Clean Up Diagram. Do it — a tidy diagram is much easier to debug. Then Ctrl+S.
7.3 Run in this order
1.	Loopback first. Jumper the Moxa's TX+ to RX+ and TX− to RX−, nothing else connected. Run, click Set Mode. TX Hex and RX Hex should both show EB 90 00 01 41 3B, and Frames RX should increment. This proves the port config, the framing, the checksum and the parser all at once, with no hardware risk.
2.	Remove the jumper, connect the A, motors unpowered. Click Set Mode. You should get EB 90 10 01 41 back.
3.	Click Read Positions. The four Current n (deg) values should sit near zero. If they're wild, flip the endianness in 6.1.
4.	Power the motors with a current limit set. Click Home All.
5.	Set Fin 1 (deg) to 1.0, tick only Move Fin 1?, click Move Fins. One fin should move.
6.	Flip Status Streaming on. Frames RX should climb steadily and Checksum Errors should stay at zero.
7.	Set Test Type to Step, tick Test Fin 1?, set Test Deg 1, click Start Test. Watch Test Chart.
7.4 If a step fails
Symptom	Look at
TX Hex wrong	The Build Array nodes — is Concatenate Inputs ticked on both?
Nothing in RX Hex	Moxa in RS-485 mode, or the TX/RX pairs aren't crossed
RX Hex shows data but Frames RX stays 0	Checksum span — it must include both EB and 90
Checksum Errors climbing fast	Termination char wasn't set to FALSE
Positions in the millions	You skipped the Subtract 8388608
All fins move together	The FALSE case of the Move Fins loop isn't wired to 0,0,0
Loop 2 won't stop	STOP is still on latch action

